    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Thanks for that. Could you expand more on the scalability/granularity piece? I'm worried that if the collection grows significantly, especially with very niche philological or local Belgian historical papers, one system might become too cluttered or difficult to manage compared to the other.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The scalability and granularity of both classification systems can be an issue, especially for specialized and niche collections. The Library of Congress Classification (LCC) traditionally features more detailed topics at the lower levels, offering a higher degree of granularity, but this can sometimes result in an overly dense structure. Conversely, the Dewey Decimal Classification (DDC) standard scheme originally had not many fine-measure classes that covered very niche topics, especially in mid-classifications that appeared at 800s level that does not give much readability for deep and narrow interpretation.",
